T-F1-SUMMARY (230 characters) [a12dc2d01d60] The audit row of a refused request records its path relative to the router when a mounted router answers: a viewer's refused GET /api/setup/entra-provisioning is logged with path / so the audit does not say which route was refused
T-F1-P1 [84e9a8d37c0a] BLUF: pull request 201 writes one REQUEST_DENIED audit row for every 401 or 403 and records the path as req.path (backend/services/accessAudit.js line 50), the field the STATE_MUTATION row already records (backend/server.js line 540). Express strips the mount prefix from the request while a router mounted with app.use answers, and does not restore it when the router ends the response without calling next, so a refusal answered inside such a router is recorded with the path relative to the router. The independent review measured, on a real server with sign-in enforced, a viewer's refused GET /api/setup/entra-provisioning recorded with path / and a viewer's refused PATCH /api/feedback/6 recorded with path /6 (its STATE_MUTATION row also reads /6, as on main before the change). The audit then does not say which route was refused. The review named three affected places: the Entra provisioning router's 403 for a viewer on any method, the check ahead of the feedback router for PATCH and DELETE on one item, and the check ahead of the sustainability router for POST on its config.
T-F1-P2 [83090d3ca294] Recommendation: record the full path in both rows, from the mount prefix plus the path (req.baseUrl plus req.path) or from the path part of req.originalUrl (never the query string), in one place used by the REQUEST_DENIED details and by the STATE_MUTATION row, and keep the six detail keys of the REQUEST_DENIED row as they are. Add cells: a viewer's refused request to each of the three places above writes a row whose path is the full path; a request answered below no router keeps the path it has now; the STATE_MUTATION row of a refused write carries the same full path; and the row still holds no header, cookie, query or body value. Red-first: put req.path back on a copy and see those cells fail. Medium: the audit still records the method, the status, the reason, the role and the masked email; what it lacks is the route, for refusals inside three routers. No decision is needed from the reader.
T-F1-P3 [abda3b96383c] Where (code main 6335717d): backend/services/accessAudit.js:50 reads path: req.path in denialDetails; backend/server.js:540 reads path: req.path in the STATE_MUTATION row; backend/routes/entraProvisioning.js:165 to :167 answers the viewer's 403 inside the router, which backend/server.js:22146 mounts at /api/setup/entra-provisioning; backend/server.js:22108 mounts the feedback router behind a check function that begins at line 22100; backend/server.js:22188 mounts the sustainability router behind a check function that begins at line 22180. Mechanism, as the independent review measured it in-process with no socket (a reading of the review, not re-run here): req.url reads /, /scim-token and /6 when the response ends, because the router restores it only in next. Not measured: the fix, any hosted request, whether other audit rows written inside a mounted router (for example the Entra router's own audit call at line 166 of its file) have the same shape, and how many routes a viewer can be refused on inside a mounted router beyond the three the review named.
T-F1-P4 [543cdd5bf74d] Acceptance (proposed): (1) with sign-in enforced and first run finished, a viewer's refused request to each of the three places writes a REQUEST_DENIED row and, for a write, a STATE_MUTATION row, each carrying the full path; (2) a refusal below no router writes the same path as before; (3) the cells are red when the change is removed, measured on a copy; (4) the suite counts are regenerated by the project's own command. Related, not duplicates: RD-932 (the audit; it stays open), RD-929 (the permission core). No Jira link is created by this note. [AUDITPATH-201-15NOV]
